{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And we shall live and not die.</b> The meaning of וְנִחְיֶה is: we shall live a life without distress, and his saying <b>וְלֹא נָמוּת</b> means, at the very least we shall not die, even if they live a life of distress." "Or it may intend by saying <b>וְלֹא נָמוּת</b> to be precise: but if they do not go, you should not say that they will merely lack a sufficient livelihood alone, but rather even a life of distress they will not live — rather they are doomed to die." "<b>Or</b> it may intend: that if they do not go, they are destined to die in the supernal world, for HaShem will say to them: Why did you not exert yourselves for the life of your souls?" "<b>Also we.</b> His saying <b>גַם</b> means: besides Benjamin, over whom you are afraid on this journey, he shall live and also they; and other than this, behold he and they will surely die, and it is not fitting that they should all die, and also Benjamin, because of a mere doubt concerning Benjamin. Even if the doubt of danger to Benjamin is to be outweighed, as stated in Jacob's argument, as I explained in the verse (Genesis 42:38): ״כִּי אָחִיו מֵת״." "And his saying <b>גַם אַתָּה</b> after mentioning the life of their father alongside their own life — it is possible that they intended to speak according to the law (Yoreh De'ah 251) that a person's life takes precedence even over his father's life. Or perhaps they intended to speak in the manner of לֹא זוֹ אַף זוֹ, and that is the higher one. Or it may say this way: not they alone, by their going, shall live, but also you need this thing, for there is no bread in all the land except in Egypt, and he is endangered if they do not go." "And the verse's saying <b>also our little ones</b> at the end — for a man's children are dearer to him than his own body. Go forth and learn from David, who said concerning Absalom, even though he was pursuing his father (II Samuel 19:1): ״מִי יִתֵּן מוּתִי״ and so forth."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 43, :notes {4 "Yoreh De'ah 251 is a section of Shulchan Aruch dealing with laws of charity and redeeming captives, which includes the principle of precedence in saving lives. The phrase לֹא זוֹ אַף זוֹ ('not this, even that') is a Talmudic idiom of escalating severity; 'the higher one' appears to mean the more stringent of the two readings."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:48:25.547360270Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}